  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Convolution",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert $y(t)=x(t)*h(t) into Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega) to represent a convolution-based LTI system output in the Fourier domain$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Convolution",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Fourier Transform of a Convolution",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When an LTI output is written as $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$, the Fourier-transform label for that output is $Y(j\\omega)$. Read $Y(j\\omega)$ as the output spectrum of the same signal, not as a new signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t)*h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To move from the time domain to the frequency domain, apply the convolution theorem to that same output. The output spectrum is still labeled $Y(j\\omega)$, and it matches the product form of the input and impulse-response spectra.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{y(t)\\}=Y(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the same LTI output has two matching views: the time-domain convolution $y(t)$ and the Fourier-domain spectrum $Y(j\\omega)$. The spectrum keeps the output label and simply changes the representation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In later sections, you will read the same mapping from equivalent convolution forms, including reversed factors and the expanded convolution integral. The rule stays the same: convolution in time becomes multiplication in frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Convolution",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Transforming $y(t)=x(t)*h(t) into the Output Spectrum$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given an LTI output $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$, write the Fourier-domain expression for $Y(j\\omega)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the convolution form $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$. Since the output is already written as an LTI convolution, the right move is to read it in the Fourier domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t)*h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Applying the convolution theorem keeps the same output label and turns the time-domain output into the product of the input and impulse-response spectra. The Fourier-domain output is $Y(j\\omega)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the transformed output is $Y(j\\omega)$, and its frequency-domain form is exactly the product $X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$. That is the Fourier-domain representation of the same LTI output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Convolution",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Transforming $h(t)$*$x(t)$ into the Same Product Spectrum",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $y(t)=h(t)*x(t)$, write the Fourier transform of the output.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the reversed convolution order $y(t)=h(t)*x(t)$. Because convolution is commutative, this is the same LTI output written with the factors swapped.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=h(t)*x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the same convolution theorem to the reordered output. The Fourier-domain output label stays $Y(j\\omega)$, and the spectrum is still the product of the input and impulse-response spectra.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So swapping the order of the time-domain factors does not change the output spectrum. The same LTI output is still represented by the product $X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$ in the Fourier domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Convolution",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Convolution Integral into a Product Spectrum",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$, write the Fourier-domain expression for the output.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the integral form of the output: it already matches the standard convolution pattern with $x(\\tau)$ and $h(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read that integral as $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$, then apply the convolution theorem to move the same output into the Fourier domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is the product spectrum for the same LTI output, now represented by $Y(j\\omega)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Convolution",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Identifying $X(j\\omega)$$H(j\\omega)$ as the Fourier Transform of $x(t)$*$h(t)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$, identify the corresponding time-domain output $y(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the product spectrum $X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$. Read that product as the Fourier-domain form of a convolution output, not as pointwise multiplication in time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the matching time-domain signal is the LTI output $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$. This is the same output written in the time domain instead of the Fourier domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t)*h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The product spectrum and the convolution output are two views of the same system response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
